// Practice quiz: Pipes and grep
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/linux-foundations/pipes-and-grep

::o1 what a pipe passes::In history | grep ssh, what does the pipe | pass to grep? {
=The standard output of history, which grep reads as its standard input #Correct. A plain pipe connects the standard output (stdout) of the command on its left to the standard input (stdin) of the command on its right; error messages on stderr are not passed.
~The name of a file called history #Not quite. A pipe passes output text, not a file name; no file is involved.
~The exit status of history #Not quite. A pipe passes the standard output stream, not the success or failure status.
~Nothing until grep has finished #Not quite. Data flows left to right; grep reads what history writes.
}

::o1 order of stages::Which pipeline prints the lines of names.txt that contain sam? {
=cat names.txt | grep sam #Correct. cat prints the file, and grep keeps the lines that contain sam.
~grep sam | cat names.txt #Not quite. Here grep has no file and waits for input, and cat ignores what flows in from the left.
~cat names.txt grep sam #Not quite. Without a pipe, cat treats grep and sam as more file names to print.
~names.txt | grep sam #Not quite. A pipe connects commands; names.txt is a file, not a command.
}

::o2 ignore case::notes.txt has lines with Todo, TODO and todo. Which command shows all of them? {
=grep -i todo notes.txt #Correct. -i ignores case, so all three spellings match.
~grep todo notes.txt #Not quite. grep matches exact case by default, so it finds only the lowercase todo.
~grep -v todo notes.txt #Not quite. -v inverts the match and keeps the lines without todo.
~grep TODO notes.txt #Not quite. Exact case again: only the uppercase TODO matches.
}

::o2 invert the match::Which command shows every line of server.log except the DEBUG lines? {
=grep -v DEBUG server.log #Correct. -v keeps the lines that do not contain the pattern.
~grep DEBUG server.log #Not quite. This keeps only the DEBUG lines.
~grep -i DEBUG server.log #Not quite. -i ignores case but still keeps the matching lines.
~grep -v server.log DEBUG #Not quite. The pattern comes before the file, so grep would search a file named DEBUG.
}

::o3 count matches::Which command prints how many lines of server.log contain WARN? {
=grep WARN server.log | wc -l #Correct. grep keeps the WARN lines, then wc -l counts the lines it receives.
~wc -l server.log | grep WARN #Not quite. wc -l runs first and outputs one count line, which contains no WARN.
~grep -v WARN server.log | wc -l #Not quite. -v keeps the other lines, so this counts the lines without WARN.
~grep WARN server.log #Not quite. This prints the lines themselves; pipe them into wc -l to get a number.
}
